WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.610 --> 00:00:06.860
As part of unit testing, Python has several
assert methods one can use to help test the

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:09.010
functionality of a unit of code.

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:14.450
Unit testing, as we have seen, helps determine,
for a block of code, whether a tester is or

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:18.619
is not getting an anticipated result for that
block of code.

00:00:18.619 --> 00:00:25.130
There are several different types of unit
tests, five of which are in this 534-assert

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:26.650
file.

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:31.010
Assert tests compare two values and return
either a true or false, depending upon the

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:32.010
comparison.

00:00:32.010 --> 00:00:39.200
This code block is a continuation of the code
we used in the topic on unit tests.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:45.579
We have the unittest library imported on line
1, and a class named TestMain that uses the

00:00:45.579 --> 00:00:51.890
built-in TestCase function on line 3.

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:57.199
We previously ran this assertEqual test on
lines 5 through 8.

00:00:57.199 --> 00:01:03.000
Thus, let's focus on some of these other assert
methods.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:12.020
Lines 10 and 11 show an assertTrue test, a
test that asserts whether a statement is true.

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:20.250
On lines 13 through 16, we see an assertIs
test, which tests whether two variables use

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:23.730
the same memory space.

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:34.790
Lines 18 through 21 run an assertIn test to
see if one object is part of a list of objects.

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:40.100
Lines 23 through 25 test to see if a variable
is an instance of a class.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:48.719
I have these lines commented out as classes
are beyond the scope of this exam, but assertIsInstance

00:01:48.719 --> 00:01:53.520
is mentioned in the exam objectives, so I
wanted you to see an example of that type

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.939
of unit test.

00:01:55.939 --> 00:02:03.180
How many of these tests do you expect to fail
in looking through these test conditions?

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:05.090
Let’s find out.

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:13.690
I will run this code, and one test failed,
the assertTrue test.

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:14.700
Why?

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:21.769
2 + 5 * 3 is not 21.

00:02:21.769 --> 00:02:27.410
Remember, the order of operations dictates
that multiplication occurs before addition.

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:34.489
So, the answer to the math problem on line
11 is 17, not 21.

00:02:34.489 --> 00:02:47.870
If we add parentheses around the addition
portion and test again, all tests will succeed.

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:53.040
In a real-life situation, you can use these
assertions to ensure a game flows the way

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:54.190
it should.

00:02:54.190 --> 00:03:00.080
From a practical sense, you can have several
assert-type tests built and then use them

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:05.620
to plug in variables as needed to see if one’s
assertions about code are true and if not,

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:11.940
one can more easily isolate, diagnose, and
fix the problem, often a logic problem, with

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:13.980
the code.

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:18.830
We have reached the end of this section on
unit testing, so it is time for another code

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:21.130
analysis exercise.

